{
  "description": "This frame captures a detailed section of a Bengali physics lecture focused on the relationship between focal length and radius of curvature in a convex mirror (উত্তল দর্পণে). The title translates to 'Convex Mirror Ray Diagram/Image' (উত্তল দর্পণে রশ্মিচিত্র/প্রতিবিম্ব). The illustration primarily focuses on defining key distances along the principal axis.\n\nKey elements shown are:\n1.  **Principal Axis:** A horizontal line representing the axis of symmetry.\n2.  **Convex Mirror:** A rightward-facing curve (and an initial incomplete one on the left).\n3.  **Key Points:** Points P (Pole, where the axis meets the mirror), F (Focus), and C (Center of Curvature) are marked on the virtual side of the mirror (the side where light does not originate).\n4.  **Distances:** Red curved lines denote the distances CP (Radius of Curvature, R) and FP (Focal Length, f).\n5.  **Annotations:** Small, numbered red arrows (1 through 6) are positioned along the axis between C and P, possibly indicating divisions of the distance or potential points for ray tracing examples.\n6.  **Formulas:** Algebraic definitions are provided: $CP = R$ (Radius of Curvature) and $FP = f$ (Focal Length). The critical derived relationship, $f = R/2$ (The focal length is half the radius of curvature), is prominently displayed within a red box.\n\nThis visual aids the student in understanding the standard nomenclature (P, F, C, f, R) and the fundamental geometric relationship governing spherical mirrors.",
